===Etymology 2===
{{etymid|ja|ui}}
{{ja-kanjitab|うい|yomi=k}}

{{IPAchar|/uɸi/}} → {{IPAchar|/uwi/}} → {{IPAchar|/ui/}}

First attested in the {{w|Kokin Wakashū}} of roughly 905-914 {{CE}},<ref>{{R:Nihon Kokugo Daijiten 2}}</ref> and assumed to be inherited from {{inh|ja|ojp|-|sort=うい}}.

There is an apparently related term {{m|ja|[[初#Japanese:_ubu|初]]|tr=ubu}} of overlapping synonymous meaning, first attested in the early 1000s. It is unclear how these two terms are related: one might be a derivation of the other, or the two might derive separately via other processes.

Both ''ui'' and ''ubu'' appear to be related to verb {{m|ja|生む|tr=umu||to [[birth]]}}, from the sense of "first of something since one has been born".

Martin suggests that this might be a fusion of {{com|ja|生む|tr1=umu|t1=to [[birth]]|日|tr2=hi|t2=[[day]]|lit=birthday}}.<ref>{{R:ja:Martin 1987}}</ref> However, the meaning of any such compound would be the noun {{m|en|birthday}} with a sense of "the day a woman gives birth" rather than "the day someone is born". Also, phonologically, this would be expected to result in ''ubi'': {{IPAchar|/umu/}} + {{IPAchar|/pi/}} → {{IPAchar|/umupi/}} → {{IPAchar|/umpi/}} → {{IPAchar|/ubi/}}.

Possibly cognate with {{m|ja|上|tr=ue||[[above]]|pos=possibly via the sense of "upstream → earlier"}} or classical verb {{m|ja|生ふ|pos=ancient ''opu'', classical ''ofu'', modern ''ou'', “to [[grow]]”}}.

