==Old Japanese==

===Etymology===
The variation between ''turugi₁'' or ''turuki₁'' suggests a compound origin. Alternatively, it may possibly be a misspelling of ''turugi₁'', as there was not a phonemic distinction of voiced and voiceless consonants in most Old Japanese texts. However, ''turuki₁'' is attested in the ''Nihon Shoki''.

A surface analysis suggests that this might be a compound of {{m|ojp|[[釣る]], [[吊る]]|tr=turu||to [[hang]], as at one's [[side]]}} + ''ki'', but there is no clear [[etymon]] for the ''ki'' portion. One possibility would be {{m|ojp|牙|tr=KI|t=[[fang]]}}, in spite that ''KI'' is not attested phonographically in Old Japanese. Such usage might parallel the combined {{m|en|tooth}} and {{m|en|blade}} meanings of the term ''ha'', spelled more specifically as {{m|ja|歯||tooth}} and {{m|ja|刃||blade}}, with these two senses listed as [[cognate]]s in Japanese dictionaries.<ref name="DJR">{{R:Daijirin}}</ref><ref name="KDJ">{{R:Kokugo Dai Jiten}}</ref>

More tentative suggestions have been connections to Austronesian, such as {{cog|tl|suligi||[[dart]], short [[spear]]}} {{rfv-etym|ja}}, but such possibilities seem only [[speculative]] at present.

